WEBVTT

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:04.675
That morning in Qatar, my microscope was sitting on

00:00:04.675 --> 00:00:08.725
the desk. The photograph was posted in July 2017.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.657
I had bought the microscope near Qasr El Einy in

00:00:14.657 --> 00:00:18.053
Cairo. Bringing it back to Qatar was a considerable

00:00:18.053 --> 00:00:18.519
hassle.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:28.604
Once it was home, I could return to an observation

00:00:28.604 --> 00:00:31.622
whenever a question interested me. I wanted that

00:00:31.622 --> 00:00:32.125
freedom.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:42.864
University laboratories offered more sophisticated

00:00:42.864 --> 00:00:46.337
instruments and formal protocols. My own microscope

00:00:46.337 --> 00:00:48.856
served a different, personal purpose.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.536
This was a vacation morning, with books and notes

00:00:55.536 --> 00:00:58.361
beside the microscope. It was not an assigned

00:00:58.361 --> 00:00:59.051
experiment.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:09.127
I could look again, change the angle, and take

00:01:09.127 --> 00:01:11.621
another day to think about what I had seen.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.392
The instrument could enlarge an object. But a

00:01:22.392 --> 00:01:25.780
persuasive explanation would still need controls and

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.213
careful documentation.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.702
The microscope lived among ordinary things on my

00:01:36.702 --> 00:01:40.282
desk. I used it to follow questions outside scheduled

00:01:40.282 --> 00:01:41.363
laboratory work.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:49.596
I carried it from Cairo to Qatar. Years later, I

00:01:49.596 --> 00:01:53.059
still remember what having an instrument at home made

00:01:53.059 --> 00:01:53.647
possible.

